list_for_each_entry_safe(of_entry, tmp, &mfd_of_node_list, list)
Reason:
These three operations—iterating, adding, and deleting entries on the global list `mfd_of_node_list`—were performed without any mutual exclusion. When multiple threads concurrently probe or remove MFD devices, they can race to modify or read the same list, causing list corruption, crashes, or undefined behavior.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
static DEFINE_MUTEX(mfd_of_node_mutex);
scoped_guard(mutex, &mfd_of_node_mutex) {
    list_for_each_entry(of_entry, &mfd_of_node_list, list)
        if (of_entry->np == np)
            return -EAGAIN;
}
scoped_guard(mutex, &mfd_of_node_mutex) {
    list_for_each_entry_safe(of_entry, tmp, &mfd_of_node_list, list)
        if (of_entry->dev == &pdev->dev) {
            list_del(&of_entry->list);
            kfree(of_entry);
        }
}
Reason:
A dedicated mutex `mfd_of_node_mutex` is defined and used with `scoped_guard` to protect all accesses to `mfd_of_node_list`. This ensures that only one thread can iterate, add, or delete entries at a time, preventing race conditions and maintaining list integrity.

